    id: "army-fitness-before-screening",
    title: "How to Build Fitness Before Nigerian Army Screening",
    slug: "how-to-build-fitness-before-nigerian-army-screening",
    excerpt:
      "Learn how to build fitness before Nigerian Army screening with a steady routine that improves stamina, strength, and consistency.",
    content:
      "If you want to build fitness before Nigerian Army screening, the most effective plan is usually a steady one. Many candidates make the mistake of waiting too long, then trying to force results with extreme exercise. That approach often leads to fatigue, inconsistency, or injury rather than real readiness.\n\nGood screening fitness is less about dramatic effort and more about discipline over time.\n\n## Start with consistency, not intensity\n\nThe first goal should be to train regularly enough that your body adapts well. When candidates jump from inactivity into hard workouts, they often become sore, discouraged, or irregular.\n\nInstead, build around a routine you can repeat.\n\nThat usually means:\n\n- training several times each week\n- increasing effort gradually\n- giving your body time to recover\n- focusing on sustainable improvement\n\nConsistency creates the base that intensity can build on later.\n\n## Build stamina first\n\nA lot of candidates benefit from improving endurance early. Better stamina helps you handle physical activity, waiting, movement, and the general pressure of recruitment preparation.\n\nGood stamina work can include:\n\n- brisk walking\n- jogging\n- short run intervals\n- regular cardio sessions\n\nThe aim is to become more comfortable with sustained effort, not to exhaust yourself every session.\n\n## Add bodyweight strength work\n\nBasic strength matters too. Bodyweight training is often a practical starting point because it builds control, discipline, and functional readiness without requiring special equipment.\n\nA simple approach can include:\n\n- push-up progressions\n- squats\n- planks\n- lunges\n- mobility drills\n\nStart from your current level and improve steadily instead of comparing yourself to others.\n\n## Protect recovery and sleep\n\nFitness improvement does not happen only during exercise. Recovery matters. Candidates who ignore sleep, hydration, and rest often perform worse even when they feel like they are training hard.\n\nSupport your fitness work with:\n\n- regular sleep\n- hydration\n- rest between demanding sessions\n- realistic progression instead of daily overexertion\n\nTraining harder is not always the same as training better.\n\n## Avoid the last-minute rush\n\nLast-minute training plans are one of the biggest mistakes candidates make. If you only become serious a few days before screening, you are more likely to feel tired than ready.\n\nA better plan is to begin early, stay steady, and keep building from there.\n\n## How Fit4Force Can Help\n\nFit4Force helps you train with more structure so your fitness preparation becomes more consistent and less random. That kind of routine is especially helpful when you are preparing for a process as demanding as Nigerian Army recruitment.\n\nYou can also explore more preparation guides on the [Fit4Force blog](/blog) while you keep checking the official Nigerian Army recruitment source for the latest screening guidance.\n\n---\n\n**Ready to build fitness the smart way?**\nDownload Fit4Force free on [Google Play](https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.fitf4force.fit_4_force) and strengthen your Nigerian Army preparation with better routine and consistency.",
