    title: "Is Nigerian Military Recruitment Free?",
    slug: "is-nigerian-military-recruitment-free",
    excerpt:
      "Learn whether Nigerian military recruitment is free and why payment requests are one of the clearest warning signs of a recruitment scam.",
    content:
      "Yes, genuine Nigerian military recruitment should be treated as free unless an official notice clearly states otherwise through the proper official channel. In practice, unsolicited payment demands are one of the clearest warning signs of a recruitment scam.\n\nThat is why candidates should be extremely careful when money enters the conversation.\n\n## Why this question matters so much\n\nMany scams target anxious applicants by pretending there is a fee for registration, shortlist placement, screening access, or final selection. These claims often sound urgent and convincing.\n\nBut pressure is exactly what scammers rely on.\n\n## Common fake payment claims\n\nBe careful if anyone says you must pay for:\n\n- form access\n- shortlist placement\n- screening confirmation\n- guaranteed success\n- insider processing\n\nThese are major red flags when they do not come from a clear official process.\n\n## The safer rule\n\nIf money is being requested through a private contact, unofficial link, or rushed conversation, do not treat it as legitimate.\n\nAlways go back to the official recruitment source and verify first.\n\n## How Fit4Force Can Help\n\nFit4Force helps candidates stay focused on preparation instead of panic-based decisions. When your routine is organized, you are less likely to fall for pressure tactics and fake recruitment shortcuts.\n\nYou can also explore more anti-scam guidance on the [Fit4Force blog](/blog).\n\n---\n\n**Ready to prepare the right way?**\nDownload Fit4Force free on [Google Play](https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.fitf4force.fit_4_force) and build your preparation around discipline, not rumor.",
